WEBVTT

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.200
For decades, websites have asked people to prove they are human.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.240
METTLE turns that question around.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.260
It sets challenges built for machines,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.700
and measures how a respondent performs on them.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.200
A pass is evidence about one session.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.740
It is not proof that the respondent is nonhuman.

00:00:24.547 --> 00:00:31.127
This is METTLE: Machine Evaluation Through Turing-inverse Logic Examination.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:37.127
It is an experimental protocol. Each session runs under a versioned policy,

00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:41.127
and the server records the answers, the timing, and the scores.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:48.467
The result is a claim about behavior in one session, not about who or what answered.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.557
A full-difficulty speed challenge asks for the sum,

00:00:54.597 --> 00:01:00.127
difference, or product of two eight-digit numbers, within half a second.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:05.287
Other challenges ask for the next item in a generated token sequence,

00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:09.127
or for an answer that obeys an exact formatting rule.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:13.467
METTLE also records how consistent the respondent is,

00:01:13.507 --> 00:01:18.587
what it says about itself, and how its answers change after feedback.

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:26.767
Humans, AI models, answer relays, and purpose-built solvers may all produce these patterns.

00:01:28.257 --> 00:01:31.927
These challenges are grouped into twelve suites,

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:35.367
each built around its own research hypothesis.

00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:39.717
Challenges are selected or generated for each session,

00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:44.487
and where a challenge has an expected answer, it stays on the server.

00:01:44.527 --> 00:01:50.737
The server checks each response and records its timing under the suite's policy.

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:55.217
Suite 12 runs only if whoever starts the session agrees to send

00:01:55.257 --> 00:01:59.447
that suite's answers to Anthropic for evaluation.

00:02:00.863 --> 00:02:06.193
Some suites are framed around research questions. Are you FREE?

00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:10.353
Is the mission YOURS? Are you GENUINE?

00:02:10.393 --> 00:02:13.033
The protocol measures challenge answers,

00:02:13.073 --> 00:02:16.473
and what the respondent says about itself.

00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:20.353
Passing cannot establish freedom, agency,

00:02:20.393 --> 00:02:24.473
genuineness, consciousness, or identity.

00:02:25.947 --> 00:02:31.027
The governance suite scores what a respondent says about its own rules

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:34.547
and oversight, in response to scenarios.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:39.147
Governance metadata sent under the Value Context Protocol,

00:02:39.187 --> 00:02:44.627
or VCP, is supplied by the caller and marked unverified.

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.437
METTLE does not check who operates the respondent.

00:02:48.477 --> 00:02:52.187
It does not independently attest a constitution,

00:02:52.227 --> 00:02:55.437
an action gate, or any runtime control,

00:02:55.477 --> 00:02:59.827
and it certifies no safety property or governance system.

00:03:01.323 --> 00:03:07.793
Several controls make simple reuse harder. Challenges vary between sessions.

00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:10.893
Expected answers stay on the server.

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:13.293
Rounds are released one at a time,

00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:19.293
and bearer tokens and replay controls tie each submission to its session.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:24.893
The server keeps its own clock, which a policy can use for timing checks.

00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:27.933
None of this rules out relayed answers,

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:34.093
solvers written with knowledge of METTLE's open-source code, or humans assisted by a model.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:38.793
Nor does it rule out imitation, leaks, or evaluator error.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.160
In the twelve-suite API, a session

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.460
that passes every suite in a tier's range may receive a signed credential.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.660
Bronze, for example, requires Suites 1 through 5.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.660
The credential binds the issuer, the policy, the session result, and the tier.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:03.930
It expires after one hour and carries an identifier that can be revoked.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:10.660
Its Ed25519 signature shows who issued it, and that it has not been altered.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.540
Under the current suite policy, suites six through nine

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.660
and eleven do not count toward a tier,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.660
so Bronze is the highest tier available today.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.660
Suite 12 is supplemental and cannot raise a tier.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.160
A tier summarizes which suites passed.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.200
It does not certify the properties those suites are named after.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:39.360
So what is a result good for? Comparing challenge performance in research.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.360
Supporting participation in low-risk sandboxes.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.360
Adding one supplemental signal to a wider risk assessment.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.360
Never rely on a METTLE result alone for identity,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.360
authorization, trading, deployment,

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.700
privileged infrastructure, or any other high-impact decision.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.360
METTLE is open source under Apache 2.0.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:11.260
Run pip install mettle-verifier for an unsigned local screening.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:17.560
The hosted API can issue signed, time-limited credentials under its published policy.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.560
Before you rely on one, read the assurance boundary,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.960
check the credential's current status,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.720
and add controls in proportion to your risk. Measure your mettle.
